{
 "summary": "Adds global network traffic accounting in `CConnman` across node lifetime using a lock-free multidimensional array of atomic counters (`NetStats`). Exposes message count and byte totals broken down by direction, network protocol, connection type, and message type via a new `detailed` field in the `getnettotals` RPC.",
 "problem": "Per-message network traffic statistics are currently only tracked on individual `CNode` instances and discarded when peers disconnect. Node operators and researchers cannot observe cumulative bandwidth consumption across message types, connection types, and network types over the node's uptime without continuous polling.",
 "evidence": [
  "Implements feature request #26337: 'Add per message stats to getnettotals rpc'.",
  "Follows up on previous attempts #27534 and #28926 that sought to introduce network message statistics."
 ],
 "dependencies": {
  "depends_on": [],
  "enables": []
 },
 "scope_notes": "Implements accounting through fixed-size nested `std::array` instances indexed by enums with `std::atomic_uint64_t` counters to avoid mutex contention on socket send/receive paths.",
 "changed_since_previous": "Switched from exposing statistics through a dedicated new `getnetmsgstats` RPC command to nesting them within a new `detailed` field inside the existing `getnettotals` RPC.",
 "card": "Adds global network message statistics collection to `CConnman` and exposes it through a new `detailed` field in the `getnettotals` RPC. Previously, per-message traffic stats existed only on active peer objects and were lost on disconnection, leaving operators without aggregate visibility into lifetime bandwidth usage by message type, network, and connection type. This resolves issue #26337 following earlier PRs #27534 and #28926. Counters use a static multidimensional array of atomics to prevent lock contention on the P2P hot path.",
